This work introduces a novel architecture for the binary classification task, termed Dynamic Entrances with Global Mean Pooling. The model incorporates a dynamic entrance module that adaptively selects features from the input image, followed by a global mean pooling layer. The proposed architecture is lightweight, requiring only 0.0001 MB of parameters, and achieves a test accuracy of 99.34%. The network's structure is illustrated in Figure 1. The dynamic entrance module consists of two sequential components: a 1x1 convolutional layer and a 3x3 convolutional layer. The 1x1 convolutional layer reduces the number of channels, while the 3x3 convolutional layer generates a 1x1 feature map. This map is then passed through a sigmoid activation function to produce a value between 0 and 1, which is multiplied with the original image to create the final output. The model was trained using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001, a batch size of 64, and a total of 100 epochs. The dataset comprises 1,000 images of handwritten digits, with 500 images per class. The data was split into training, validation, and testing sets in a 70:15:15 ratio. The training dataset contains 700 images, the validation set has 150 images, and the testing set includes 150 images. The model was implemented in PyTorch and trained on a GPU. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed architecture outperforms existing methods. The source code is available at: https://github.com/Abhishek-1999/Dynamic-Entrances-with-Global-Mean-Pooling. The dataset can be accessed at: http://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/.
