We introduce a novel method to detect minute variations in local magnetic fields using atomic interferometry, driven by the Meissner effect in superconductors. This approach offers a significant enhancement in sensitivity compared to conventional atomic interferometric techniques. The core of our method involves creating a local magnetic field gradient via the Meissner effect, which in turn generates a local rotation of the atomic wavefunction's spin. This rotation is then converted into a measurable phase shift through the spin-dependent Aharonov-Bohm effect. We validate this technique experimentally by observing the phase shift caused by the local magnetic field gradient produced by a superconducting lead (Pb) film. The resulting sensitivity reaches 1.1 nT/$\sqrt{\mathrm{Hz}}$ at a 100 s integration time, representing a 100-fold improvement over standard atomic interferometric methods. This advancement could enable the detection of extremely weak magnetic fields from distant sources, such as those associated with dark matter. Furthermore, this method could serve as a precise sensor for local magnetic field fluctuations, potentially identifying magnetic impurities in superconducting materials. The proposed technique is compatible with various atomic interferometric configurations, including Mach-Zehnder, Sagnac, and Ramsey-Bordé setups. Additionally, it can be applied to other scenarios where local magnetic field gradients arise from superconducting materials, such as Josephson junctions and SQUIDs. Ultimately, this method could facilitate the development of a new class of atomic interferometric sensors specifically designed to detect local magnetic field alterations induced by superconductors. This work was supported by JSPS KAKENHI Grant Number 21H04904.
